🧩 Problem: Linked List Cycle (LeetCode 141)

Detect if a linked list has a cycle.

🧠 1. How to START (Brute force thinking)
💬 What you say

“One straightforward way is to track visited nodes.”

💡 Approach
Traverse list
Store each node in a HashSet
If a node repeats → cycle exists
⏱ Complexity
Time: O(n)
Space: O(n)
❌ Drawback

“Uses extra space — we can optimize this.”

🚀 2. Optimization Insight (Key jump)
💬 What you say

“Instead of storing nodes, maybe I can detect a cycle using pointer movement.”

Then lead into:

“If there's a cycle, two pointers moving at different speeds will eventually meet.”

👉 This transition is what interviewers LOVE.

⚙️ 3. Optimal Approach (Floyd’s Cycle Detection)
💡 Idea
Use two pointers:
slow → moves 1 step
fast → moves 2 steps
If cycle exists → they will meet
If no cycle → fast reaches null
🧠 Clean Explanation Flow

“I’ll use two pointers.
The slow pointer moves one step at a time, and the fast pointer moves two steps.
If there’s a cycle, the fast pointer will eventually catch up with the slow pointer.
If there’s no cycle, the fast pointer will reach the end.”

✅ Code (clean version)
public boolean hasCycle(ListNode head) {
    if (head == null) return false;

    ListNode slow = head;
    ListNode fast = head;

    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
        slow = slow.next;
        fast = fast.next.next;

        if (slow == fast) return true;
    }

    return false;
}
⏱ Complexity
Time: O(n)
Space: O(1) ✅ (key improvement)
👀 4. Visualization (IMPORTANT)
Example with cycle:
1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5
          ↑     ↓
          ← ← ←
Movement:
Step 1:
slow = 2
fast = 3

Step 2:
slow = 3
fast = 5

Step 3:
slow = 4
fast = 4  ← meet → cycle exists
🧠 5. INTUITION (this is what makes it stick)

Think of it like:

🏃 Two runners on a circular track

One slow, one fast
Fast will eventually lap slow → they meet

If no cycle:

Fast runs off the track (null)

🔁 6. Mental Model (for revision)

“Slow = 1 step, Fast = 2 steps → if cycle, they meet.”

🧱 7. Common Mistakes
Forgetting fast.next != null check → NPE
Comparing values instead of nodes (slow == fast, NOT slow.val)
Starting fast incorrectly (should be same start or slight variation, both fine)
